  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nSouthwest flow aloft continues to bring light to moderate showers\nand isolated thunderstorms. As showers move east overnight, they \nare expected to weaken. Another round of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms is expected Saturday initiating off the higher \nterrain and tracking east through the afternoon and evening. \nAlthough the mid level pattern shifts to a more westerly flow, a \nslight southwest flow across western MT will remain in place, \nallowing for afternoon scattered showers and thunderstorms again \nSunday. Thunderstorms over the weekend are not expected to be \nsevere, but a few stronger storms are possible. The best \ninstability will be for the foothill areas where 1000 J/kg or less\nof MUCAPE and 20-30 kts of bulk shear will be present. With \nprecipitable water values remaining near an inch, heavy rain will \nbe the main concern, especially over area burn scars. Into Monday,\nensembles the show a low pressure system moving through central \nand eastern Canada dragging some energy behind it, keeping low to \nmoderate chances for precipitation Monday and Tuesday. As heights\ndecrease with this system Tuesday, a frontal passage looks to \ndrop in from the north/northeast. Following this, clusters and \nensembles are in good agreement for ridging to move in Wednesday \nthrough Friday, keeping conditions warm and mostly dry.\n\nHigh temperatures today will be in the 70s today, warming into the\nupper 70s and 80s (near normal) for the remainder of the period.\nTS",
